  "notes": "All 5 events extracted from PABL's workbooks and cross-validated against each other via file-modification timestamps and row-by-row divergence analysis (each workbook contains the FULL history from original 2016 drawdown through its own version's end, so events were identified by finding the exact row index where each file's numbers first diverge from the prior version's).\n\nChronology and effective dates (confirmed via divergence-point analysis, not by filename date labels alone, since the internal \"PABL_Loan\" helper-table labels such as \"Extended Tenor-1st Restructuring\" turned out to be a repeatedly-overwritten auxiliary column header, not a reliable date index):\n1. Rescheduled.xlsx (mtime 2021-03-31): extends principal moratorium from the original 2017-06-15 start out to 2019-06-13. Divergence from the true original loan (PABL_Loan.xlsx, which fully amortizes by 2023-03-15) begins at 2017-06-15.\n2. Restructure.xlsx (mtime 2018-12-02, i.e. genuinely earlier in real time than the \"Rescheduled\" file's last save): pulls the moratorium back in, resuming principal on 2018-06-14 and fully amortizing by 2024-06-15 via annuity.\n3. Restructure_2nd.xlsx (mtime 2019-12-03): reverts to extending the moratorium again out to 2019-06-13 (essentially re-adopting the 1st Rescheduled plan), fully amortizing by 2025-03-15.\n4. Restructure_3rd (27 Apr'21).xlsx (mtime 2025-03-02, filename suggests board/BB approval ~27 Apr 2021): the borrower actually defaulted/entered an extended non-payment moratorium from 2019-09-15 (LPC + interest capitalization of 20,830,614.82 raises balance from 337,670,000 to 358,500,614.82 at that point - documented in the final file's PABL_Loan sheet as \"Total Capitalized-till rescheduling\" = 20,830,614.821) through 2020-12-15, with further capitalization of 10,019,135.92 documented as \"3rd Restructuring capitalization\" (raising balance to 406,380,557.02 by 2020-12-15). Regular level-principal installments of 16,932,523.21/quarter then resume 2021-03-15, projected to fully amortize by 2026-12-15.\n5. Rescheduled_4th (27 Feb'25).xlsx (mtime 2026-01-29): the borrower again fell into arrears - the workbook explicitly labels a \"Principal Due before 4th Reschedule\" = 270,920,371.34, \"Principal Received before 4th Reschedule\" = 80,136,086, and \"Principal overdue before 4th reschedule\" = 190,784,285.34479344 (found at PABL_Loan!AH16:AH18 in the source file). This overdue PRINCIPAL amount is rolled into the new opening balance: the closing balance that would have resulted had the last regular installment (due 2024-12-15, principal 16,932,523.21) actually been paid was 135,460,185.67; adding the overdue amount of 190,784,285.34479344 gives exactly the new opening balance of 326,244,471.01719046 used from 2025-02-13 onward (verified to the cent). No interest was capitalized into this new balance - interest accrual simply stops (rate effectively goes to 0%) and the new schedule is a monthly, interest-free, level-principal-only repayment of 24 installments of 13,593,519.625716269 each, fully closing to 0 by 2027-01-15.\n\nAll five params objects use the override arrays (opening_balance_amounts / interest_payment_amounts / closing_balance_amounts, plus principal_schedule_amounts for clarity) rather than first-principles annuity/PPMT formulas, because every version contains real-world irregularities (non-banking-day date shifts, an extended default/moratorium period with ad hoc capitalization, and a switch from quarterly annuity to monthly interest-free level-principal) that a clean formula cannot reproduce exactly - consistent with the MCML/QPSL/SCBL/SKS/THERMAX precedent in examples.ts. The interest rate stayed at 10% (0.10) unchanged across all of events 1-4; only event 4→5 (Rescheduled_4th) is effectively a RATE_CHANGE to 0% bundled with a tenor/frequency change, but since it also changes principal_type/frequency/tenor and rolls in overdue principal, it is classified as RESCHEDULE per the task's guidance (only a rate-ONLY change would be RATE_CHANGE).\n\nNo explicit regulatory circular reference (e.g. \"DFIM Circular 04/2021\") was found anywhere in the text of any of the five workbooks, so regulatoryReference is null for all events; the \"27 Apr'21\" and \"27 Feb'25\" filename dates likely correspond to Bangladesh Bank/board approval or signing dates rather than the schedule's own effective date (which is set to the first repayment-schedule row that reflects each version's new terms).\n\ncapitalizedAmount for the 3rd Restructuring event (20,830,614.82) is the disclosed \"Total Capitalized-till rescheduling\" figure from the source workbook; it could not be tied to a single exact schedule row via simple arithmetic (likely spans a multi-period LPC + interest capitalization booked with a lag), hence the reliance on override arrays rather than a derived capitalization schedule.\n\nSource files (all under \"E:/Projects/IDCOL 2.0/Loan Repayment Schedule (The Heart)/PABL/\"): PABL_Loan_Rescheduled.xlsx, PABL_Loan_Restructure.xlsx, PABL_Loan_Restructure_2nd.xlsx, PABL_Loan_Restructure_3rd_27 Apr'21.xlsx, PABL_Loan_Rescheduled_4th_27 Feb'25.xlsx. Rosetta Stone reference: E:\\Projects\\IDCOL\\2026\\Repayment Doc\\CBS-IDCOL\\frontend\\lib\\examples.ts (EX_DATA[\"PABL\"], lines 941-1009), and override-array pattern examples at EX_DATA[\"SCBL\"] (lines 2000-2236) and EX_DATA[\"SKS\"]/[\"THERMAX\"]."
